# KNOWN GAP (todo/t1225) — a BARE INDEX PLACE of single-owner type is an
# implicit second owner. `gg check` rc 0, `gg build` rc 0, and the program
# DOUBLE-FREES one closure environment under `--sanitize`.
#
# `Callable[T]` has NO implicit-copy path in the lowering, so each bare read
# binds another owner of the same env. The FIELD and TUPLE spellings of exactly
# this shape are rejected at check time (`E_MoveWithoutOperator`, see
# `tests/fixtures/single_owner_subplace/`); the INDEX spelling is the ONE
# outermost projection the gate deliberately does not key on, because rejecting
# it today forces a per-request `.clone()` of a closure env on the httpserver
# dispatch hot path — the charter breach the owner's callee-borrow ruling
# (2026-09-04: a call does not consume its callee) removes instead.
#
# ⚠ THE TWO READS ARE DELIBERATELY OF THE *SAME* ELEMENT — THAT IS THE AXIS.
# Each read of one element binds another owner of its single heap value. A
# single read is clean, and so are two reads of DISTINCT elements: add
# `v.push(mk(50))` after the push below and make the second bind `v[1]`, and
# this same program runs CLEAN (`41`/`51`, rc 0). The container's spelling is
# NOT the axis — the same same-element loop reached through a `&` BORROW PARAM
# is a use-after-free too. So `lib/xtd/httpserver.gg` is safe only because each
# of its 8 dispatch sites binds a DISTINCT key or index, and nothing enforces
# that: a refactor that reads one element twice reintroduces this silently.
#
# INTENDED: either the callee-borrow rule lands and a call-only bind is a
# BORROW (no second owner, no clone, no free), or the reject is widened to index
# places. Either way this program must not double-free. Asserted below as a
# clean run printing `41` twice.
#
# Measured at 7bf11017e: check rc 0, build rc 0, run under
# `ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1:exitcode=1` → `AddressSanitizer: attempting
# double-free`, rc 1.

Callable[int()] mk(int n):
    return (): n + 1

void main():
    Vector[Callable[int()]] v = []
    v.push(mk(40))
    Callable[int()] g = v[0]
    print(g())
    Callable[int()] g2 = v[0]
    print(g2())
